# {{ name }}

A Fastify 5 service built on [`@repo/fastify-base`](../../packages/fastify-base/README.md).

## Scripts

| Script          | What it does                                           |
| --------------- | ------------------------------------------------------ |
| `dev`           | `node --watch src/index.ts` — restarts on change.      |
| `start`         | `node src/index.ts` — production entry, no build step. |
| `test`          | Vitest, once.                                          |
| `test:watch`    | Vitest in watch mode.                                  |
| `test:coverage` | Vitest with v8 coverage, gated at 80%.                 |
| `check-types`   | `tsc`.                                                 |

Run them from the repo root with `npm run <script> -w {{ name }}`, or through turbo
(`npx turbo run test --filter={{ name }}`).

There is no `build`. Node 24 strips TypeScript types at load, so `src/index.ts` runs directly —
including from a container.

## Structure

`src/app.ts` is a plugin holding the entire stack; `src/index.ts` constructs the instance and owns
the lifecycle. Reading those two files tells you everything this service does at boot.

```
src/
  index.ts               entry point — Fastify(options), closeWithGrace, listen
  app.ts                 config + basePlugin + this service's own plugins
  plugins/               your own plugins, registered explicitly from app.ts
  routes/                the API surface; autoloaded, directory names are URL prefixes
  schemas/               Zod schemas shared between routes
{{#if postgres}}  db/
    schemas/             Drizzle table definitions
    migrations/          generated SQL — committed, never hand-edited
{{/if}}test/                    mirrors src/; never inside it
{{#if postgres}}drizzle.config.ts        drizzle-kit's view of src/db
{{/if}}```

Tests live in `test/`, not beside the code they cover. Autoload matches `foo.test.ts` as readily as
`foo.ts`, so a colocated test beside a route would be registered as a route.

**Only routes are autoloaded.** Plugins are registered by hand in `app.ts`, which is what lets them
take ordinary options — how a test substitutes a stand-in client.

`basePlugin` is the shared stack from `@repo/fastify-base`: config, Zod, security middleware, health
probes, metrics, OpenAPI, the error handler. To change one of those defaults, replace that single
line with the individual plugins it registers — that list is in the package's README, and
`expectBaseContract` in `test/app.test.ts` fails if the order comes out wrong.

Adding your own plugin means writing it in `src/plugins/` and registering it in `app.ts` too.

Plugins are wrapped in [`fastify-plugin`](https://github.com/fastify/fastify-plugin) when they need
to decorate the server for everyone else. Route plugins never are — encapsulation is what keeps one
route's hooks out of every other route.

## Environment

`loadConfig()` validates `process.env` at boot and throws on anything invalid, so a misconfigured
service fails immediately instead of at the first request. Every variable and its default is listed
in `.env.example`; copy it to `.env` to get started. `.env` is gitignored, and `process.loadEnvFile()`
tolerates it being absent, which is the normal case in CI.

To add a service-specific variable, pass it to `loadConfig`:

```ts
import { loadConfig } from "@repo/fastify-base";
import { z } from "zod";

const extraEnv = {
  DATABASE_URL: z.url(),
  MAX_RETRIES: z.coerce.number().default(3),
};

const config = loadConfig(extraEnv);

// Makes the same variables reachable as `fastify.config` from every plugin and route. Named rather
// than inlined below: oxlint's `import/namespace` cannot follow `z.infer` into a `declare module`
// block, and reports it as missing from `zod`.
type ExtraEnv = z.infer<z.ZodObject<typeof extraEnv>>;

declare module "fastify" {
  interface FastifyConfig extends ExtraEnv {}
}
```

`config` is fully typed from that call, and so is `fastify.config` — both derive from `extraEnv`, so
the runtime schema and the type cannot drift. **Coercion is explicit** — `process.env` values are
always strings, so a plain `z.number()` always fails; use `z.coerce.number()` or `z.stringbool()`.

Give a variable a default only when the fallback is genuinely safe everywhere. A connection string
is not: defaulting it to localhost turns "deployed without configuration" from a crash at boot into
a service that starts and quietly fails readiness.

