## Data stores

{{#if redis}}
[`@fastify/redis`](https://github.com/fastify/fastify-redis) dials `REDIS_URL` and decorates the
server with `server.redis`, an [ioredis](https://github.com/redis/ioredis) client that closes with
it. It **waits for the connection during registration**, so a Redis that is unreachable at boot
fails the service outright with `ERR_AVVIO_PLUGIN_TIMEOUT` rather than starting it up degraded.
`enableOfflineQueue` is off, so a command issued during a later outage rejects instead of queueing
until the connection returns — without that, a `/readyz` request would hang rather than report.

{{/if}}
{{#if postgres}}
`src/plugins/external/postgres.ts` builds a `pg` pool from `DATABASE_URL` and `PG_POOL_MAX`, hands
it to Drizzle, and decorates the server with `server.db`. The pool is lazy, so nothing connects
until the first query; `connectionTimeoutMillis` is set because pg otherwise waits forever for one,
and the plugin drains the pool on `onClose`.

{{/if}}
Each client lives in its own file under `src/plugins/external/`, registered after everything
`@repo/fastify-base` provides. Each registers a readiness probe on the way in:

```ts
fastify.addHealthCheck("postgres", () => client.query("SELECT 1"));
```

`/readyz` runs every registered probe under `HEALTH_TIMEOUT_MS` and reports 503 while any of them
rejects or hangs, recovering on its own once the store is back. Ordinary traffic is untouched —
`/ping` and `/livez` keep answering 200.

Both clients are registered from `app.ts` with ordinary plugin options, so a test substitutes a
stand-in by passing one: `register(app, { pool: stub })`. That is why `test/app.test.ts` runs with
nothing installed.

{{/if}}
{{#if postgres}}
## Database

[Drizzle](https://orm.drizzle.team) owns the pool outright. `server.db` is typed against everything
`src/db/schemas/` exports:

```ts
// src/routes/things.ts
import { items } from "../db/schemas/index.ts";

const rows = await fastify.db.select().from(items).limit(10);
```

Import schemas relatively, as everything else here does. **Do not reach for a `paths` alias** —
there is no build step, so Node resolves every specifier itself and never reads `tsconfig.json`; an
`@/db/…` import typechecks clean and then dies at boot with `ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND`. If the relative
depth ever does become worth solving, the working tool is a
[Node subpath import](https://nodejs.org/api/packages.html#subpath-imports) in `package.json`, which
must start with `#`.

There is deliberately **one** query API. Raw SQL goes through ``fastify.db.execute(sql`…`)``, and
`fastify.db.$client` is the pool itself for the rare thing neither covers. A second decorator over
the same pool — `@fastify/postgres`'s `server.pg`, say — would look convenient and quietly break
transactions: its `transact()` checks out its own client, so anything done through `fastify.db`
inside that callback runs on a different connection and escapes the rollback. Use
`fastify.db.transaction()`, which cannot have that problem:

```ts
await fastify.db.transaction(async (tx) => {
  await tx.insert(items).values({ name });
});
```

```
src/db/
  schemas/
    index.ts             the barrel drizzle-kit and the plugin both read
    example.ts           a placeholder table — replace it
  migrations/            generated SQL plus meta/_journal.json
```

Adding a table means writing it in `src/db/schemas/`, re-exporting it from `index.ts`, and
generating a migration. A table missing from the barrel is invisible to both `server.db` and
`db:generate`.

`src/db` is excluded from coverage in `vitest.config.ts`. Table definitions are data, not behaviour
— the callbacks in `$defaultFn`, `references` and `relations` are stored at import rather than run,
so v8 counts an ordinary schema as dead code and pulls `% Funcs` below the 80% gate.

| Script        | What it does                                                                       |
| ------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `db:generate` | Diffs `src/db/schemas/` against `src/db/migrations/` and writes the next SQL file. |
| `db:migrate`  | Applies pending migrations to `DATABASE_URL`.                                      |
| `db:studio`   | Opens Drizzle Studio against `DATABASE_URL`.                                       |

Run them from the repo root: `npm run db:generate -w {{ name }}`. drizzle-kit reads `DATABASE_URL`
from `.env` through `drizzle.config.ts`, which loads it the same way `loadConfig` does.

Migrations are committed. Generate them, read the SQL before committing, and never edit one that has
already been applied — write a new one instead.

**`casing: "snake_case"` is set twice**, in `drizzle.config.ts` and in the `drizzle()` call. Both
have to agree: with only one of them set, migrations create `createdAt` while queries ask for
`created_at`, and nothing catches it until runtime.

`drizzle-kit` is a devDependency, so `npm ci --omit=dev` leaves it out of the container image.
Migrating is a deploy step run from the repo — not something the service does to itself at boot,
which would race across replicas.

