## Endpoints

`GET /ping` is this service's own, from `src/routes/root.ts`. The rest come from
`@repo/fastify-base`:

| Route      | Purpose                                                                             |
| ---------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `/livez`   | Liveness. Checks no dependencies, and keeps answering 200 even while shutting down. |
| `/readyz`  | Readiness. 503 when a registered health check fails or times out.                   |
| `/metrics` | Prometheus exposition, including per-route request durations.                       |
| `/docs`    | OpenAPI UI, only when `ENABLE_DOCS=true`.                                           |

Point the load balancer at `/readyz`, not `/livez`.

## Routes

A route file default-exports a plugin, and its path under `src/routes` is its URL prefix —
`routes/api/tasks/index.ts` serves `/api/tasks`. Schemas are Zod, with request and reply types
inferred from them:

```ts
// src/routes/api/things/index.ts  →  /api/things
import type { FastifyPluginAsyncZod } from "@repo/fastify-base";
import { ThingSchema } from "../../../schemas/things.ts";

const things: FastifyPluginAsyncZod = async (fastify) => {
  fastify.post(
    "/",
    { schema: { body: ThingSchema, response: { 201: ThingSchema }, tags: ["things"] } },
    async (req, reply) => reply.code(201).send(await create(req.body)),
  );
};

export default things;
```

Do not give a route a response schema for an error status. The Zod serializer throws on a shape it
rejects instead of falling back, and the shared error handler already sends one consistent body.

To apply a hook to a whole subtree, add `autohooks.ts` beside the routes — it covers that directory
and everything nested under it:

```ts
// src/routes/api/autohooks.ts — guards every route under /api
export default async function (fastify: FastifyInstance) {
  fastify.addHook("onRequest", async (request, reply) => {
    if (!request.headers.authorization) reply.unauthorized();
  });
}
```

A schema a client also needs belongs in a shared workspace package tagged `isomorphic`, so both
sides agree on one definition rather than drifting apart.

## Boundaries

`turbo.json` tags this workspace `["app", "node"]`. Nothing may depend on an app, and a `node`
workspace may not depend on a `browser` one — `npm run boundaries` enforces both.
